sizeof a string literal counts BYTES, not the characters used to spell it.

MEASURED:

    sizeof("abc")        4    correct
    sizeof("a\nb")       5    should be 4
    sizeof("\t\t")       5    should be 3
    sizeof("\\")         3    should be 2
    sizeof("!<arch>\n")  10   should be 9

Every literal WITHOUT an escape was already right, which is why this survived:
sizeof used string_length on the raw token, and the comment there explains the
trick -- the token keeps its OPENING QUOTE and drops the closing one, so the
quote stands in for the terminating NUL. Exact for "abc". An escape, though,
is several source characters and one byte, and nothing collapsed them.

THE LAST LINE IS ARMAG. tcc starts reading an archive at

    file_offset = sizeof ARMAG - 1;                 tccelf.c:3540

so mc-tcc began ONE BYTE INTO the first member header. Probed from inside:

    AR-READ off=9 len=60 sizeof=60 fmag=0a00 name=               0

fmag came out 0a00 -- the '\n' of the magic followed by the first byte of the
name field -- instead of "`\n", and read_ar_header returned -1. mc-tcc
therefore rejected EVERY archive, including ones it had just written itself
and ones the system `ar` reads without complaint.

THE WALK IS THE ONE THE STRING EMITTER ALREADY DOES.
amount_of_escaped_chars_to_skip knows how wide each escape is -- \xNN, an
octal run, or a single character -- and this counts with it instead of
emitting. A literal with no escapes takes the same path and comes out the same
number it always did.

THREE BUGS STOOD BETWEEN mc-tcc AND A USABLE ARCHIVE, in three layers, and
each hid the next:

  1  EXPERIMENT-zzzq   an array member's alignment made sizeof(ArHdr) 64
                       instead of 60, so the header written was four bytes long
  2  m2libc 0014       fopen ignored '+', so the copy-back read nothing and
                       silently emitted stale heap
  3  this              sizeof ARMAG was 10, so the reader started one byte late

MEASURED AFTER ALL THREE:

    mc-tcc links against its own single-object archive   -> 5, correct
    mc-tcc links against its own three-object archive    -> 6, correct
    system `ar t` and `nm` read them

    difftest amd64        98 pass, 0 fail, 2 known gaps      unchanged
    difftest aarch64     100 pass, 0 fail, 2 known gaps      unchanged
    stage 2 corpus       419 of 426                          unchanged
    tests2 through mc-tcc 90 of 105                          unchanged
    self-compilation      gen2.o == gen3.o == gen4.o         unchanged

